# Environment Variables Example
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in your values.
# .env.local is gitignored — never commit real values.

# PostHog. Without the key the analytics chain never loads at all.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* is exposed to the browser by design: PostHog's client SDK
# needs the project key at runtime. Use the PROJECT API key (write-only),
# never a Personal API key — a personal key can read and modify your data.
# For production it lives in the committed .env.production, like the cursor
# URL: the Pages dashboard does not reach the build (see that file).
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=
# Optional. Defaults to /ingest, the same-origin proxy in functions/ingest.
# Set to https://us.i.posthog.com only to bypass the proxy.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=

# The other five analytics vendors (Clarity, Cloudflare, Umami, Mixpanel,
# GA4) carry their IDs inline in app/analytics/VendorScripts.tsx. Those are
# public client-side identifiers, not secrets, so they need nothing here.

# Multiplayer cursors. Points at the Durable Object Worker in worker/.
# Unset (the default) means the feature never connects and no socket code is
# ever downloaded — the page is simply single-player.
#
#   cd worker && npm install && npx wrangler deploy
#
# then put the wss:// URL it prints, with /cursors appended, in
# .env.production (committed — not the Pages dashboard, which the root
# wrangler.toml overrides), and add this site's origin to ALLOWED_ORIGINS in
# worker/wrangler.toml. Set it in .env.local only to test against a local
# `wrangler dev`. Not a secret: it is a public endpoint.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CURSORS_URL=
